# config/llama-server.env
# Configuration for the llama-server inference backend (main slot).
# This file is read by systemd's EnvironmentFile directive.
# The model manager updates MODEL_PATH when swapping models.

# Path to the GGUF model file to load on startup. Preloaded like the batch slot
# so main boots with a model and the device pin is exercised at startup.
MODEL_PATH=/opt/llama/models/Qwen3.8-27B-UD-Q6_K.gguf

# CUDA device selector. CUDA0 is the Tesla V100 32GB (GV100GL "Tesla
# PG500-216", compute capability 7.0) -- it replaced the Tesla P40 on
# 2026-09-23. Explicit + fail-loud: the service errors if CUDA0 is absent
# rather than silently falling back to the iGPU (which has no dedicated VRAM
# and OOMs the host with a large model).
#
# NOTE: CUDA0 names an enumeration position, not a card, and CUDA's default
# enumeration order is not guaranteed to match PCI bus order. That is
# unobservable with only one CUDA device installed, but once the P40 returns
# as an eGPU there are two, and CUDA0 could name either one depending on
# enumeration order at boot. CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES below is what makes CUDA0
# unambiguous again -- see the device-pinning section of
# docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-23-dual-gpu-three-slot-design.md.
DEVICE=CUDA0

# Restricts this process to exactly one physical GPU by UUID, so CUDA0 above
# always names this card regardless of enumeration order or how many other
# CUDA devices are present. Get this host's UUID with:
#   nvidia-smi --query-gpu=uuid,pci.bus_id,name --format=csv
# and set CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=GPU-<uuid> for the row that is this slot's
# card (currently the Tesla V100 / "Tesla PG500-216"). An ordinal
# (CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0) is NOT sufficient -- it is exactly the same
# enumeration-position problem as CUDA0 above, just moved one layer out.
#
# This value is specific to one physical card in one machine and is
# deliberately NOT committed here with a real UUID -- the placeholder below
# is intentionally invalid so the service fails loudly instead of silently
# landing on the wrong card if this template is deployed unedited. Fill in
# the real UUID in the deployed copy at /etc/llama/llama-server.env only.
CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=GPU-<uuid-of-this-hosts-v100-see-comment-above>

# Number of model layers to offload to GPU.
# Set to -1 for auto-detect (uses all available VRAM), or a specific number.
# The V100 has 32494 MiB; this model's weights are ~20.5 GiB, so all layers fit.
N_GPU_LAYERS=-1

# Work around a CUDA-backend leak where CUDA graphs are cached per generation
# and not freed, growing host RAM under sustained load (llama.cpp issue #20315).
# Introduced for the P40 on llama.cpp b8851. Whether it is still needed on
# b11136 with the V100 has NOT been retested -- left enabled as the
# conservative choice. Re-measure before removing it; the primary OOM fix is
# --cache-ram 0 in the unit's ExecStart, and this is a secondary guard.
GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_GRAPHS=1

# Context window size in tokens.
#
# Measured, not projected. Qwen3.8-27B is hybrid linear attention: of its 64
# layers only every 4th (full_attention_interval=4) holds a KV cache, so 16
# layers x 4 KV heads x (256+256) x 2 bytes = 64 KiB/token. Confirmed by
# loading it and reading llama-server's own accounting:
#
#   llama_kv_cache:        size = 2048.00 MiB ( 32768 cells, 16 layers, 4/1 seqs)
#   llama_memory_recurrent: size =  598.50 MiB (     4 cells, 64 layers, 4 seqs)
#
# The recurrent state is a fixed cost that scales with sequence count, not
# with context. Budget against the V100's 31.73 GiB:
#
#   weights 20.5 + KV 8.0 (131072 @ 64 KiB) + recurrent + compute ~= 29.4 GiB
#
# leaving ~2.3 GiB headroom. The model's native context is 262144, which needs
# 16 GiB of f16 KV and does NOT fit; reaching it requires q8_0 KV
# quantization (--cache-type-k/-v q8_0), which halves the KV term back to
# 8 GiB and depends on flash attention. Not enabled here yet.
#
# Re-derive this from the llama_kv_cache line after any model change rather
# than scaling the number -- `-lv 5` is required to surface that line on b11136.
CTX_SIZE=131072

# Bind address and port. Always localhost — the model manager is the public-facing endpoint.
HOST=127.0.0.1
PORT=8081
